  "narration": {
    "title": "The Bats Report: A New Home Run King Hits 40",
    "intro": "Kyle Schwarber is the first hitter to 40 home runs, and the Phillies have the leader. Yordan Alvarez still owns the best overall bat, but Pete Crow-Armstrong put on the loudest show of the week. Here's where the hitting stands as September opens.",
    "offense_lead": "The Cubs still set the pace with a .773 team OPS, a bit clear of the Nationals at .757 and the Dodgers at .756. Milwaukee and Colorado round out the top five, both at .738.",
    "bats_lead": "Yordan Alvarez holds the top spot at a 1.036 OPS and remains on historic pace, though he cooled a touch this week. Pete Crow-Armstrong went the other way, climbing to a .943 OPS while adding five homers, three of them in a single game at Cincinnati. Bryce Harper crossed into the elite tier at .901.",
    "season_leaders_lead": "Schwarber's 40 homers lead everyone, with Crow-Armstrong at 38 and a logjam at 37 behind him in Alvarez, Junior Caminero and Matt Olson. Sal Stewart paces the RBI chase with 105, and Luis Arraez tops the batting race at .317, a single point ahead of Alvarez.",
    "hr_lead": "Crow-Armstrong's five-homer week is the story here, powered by three in one game against the Reds. Sal Stewart hit three straight days against the Cubs, and Michael Harris II spread three across three different opponents."
  }
}